"Demonstrate mastery of concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry","activities":["Describe the mole concept (comparison of the mole with other units of measurement and molar quantities of substances)","Explain Avogadro's Law in relation to molar quantities"],"methods":["Outdoor learning visiting nearby shops to relate everyday units (cartons, pairs) to the mole concept, followed by classroom mass/volume-to-mole conversion assignments","Group discussion applying Avogadro's Law to calculate molar quantities"],"resources":["Analytical balance, different chemicals, videos/animations/simulations, wall charts illustrating molar mass of elements, scientific calculator"],"assessment":[],"periods":"68 (shared across this Specific Competence)"},{"topic":"Volumetric Analysis","mcCode":"1.0","mc":"Demonstrate mastery of basic concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.0 Conduct experiments in Chemistry","scCode":"1.1","sc":"Demonstrate mastery of concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.1 Conduct experiments in Chemistry","activities":["Explain the principles of volumetric analysis (preparation of standard solutions and volumetric calculations)","Describe the applications of volumetric analysis in real life and industrial settings (e.g., soft drinks, cocktail making, medicine, agriculture)","Perform experiments on volumetric analysis (titrations for various standard solutions)"],"methods":["Brainstorming on the concept of volumetric analysis, plus a project visiting food/drinks/cosmetics/pharmaceutical industries","Group discussion performing volumetric calculations and presenting results","Experimentation performing titrations, with field trips to industries concerned with quantification of species"],"resources":["Analytical balance, pipette, burette, volumetric flasks, beakers, distilled water, petri dish, watch glass, funnels, wash bottles, HCl, H2SO4, HNO3, CH3COOH, NaOH, Ca(OH)2, Na2CO3, NaHCO3; baking powder, vinegar, processed juice, antacids, fertiliser"],"assessment":[],"periods":"68 (shared, under 1.1) + 58 (shared, under 2.1)"},{"topic":"Ionic Theory and Electrolysis","mcCode":"1.0","mc":"Demonstrate mastery of basic concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.0 Conduct experiments in Chemistry","scCode":"1.1","sc":"Demonstrate mastery of concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.1 Conduct experiments in Chemistry","activities":["Explore the basic tenets of ionic theory and electrolysis (ionic theory, mechanism of electrolysis and laws of electrolysis)","Perform experiments on the applications of electrolysis in corrosion inhibition and purification of metals"],"methods":["Group discussion describing ionic theory/electrolysis concepts, with experimentation demonstrating the mechanism of electrolysis","Field trips to industries involved in extraction, electroplating and purification of metals","Experimentation investigating electrolysis in electroplating for corrosion inhibition and metal purification"],"resources":["Electrolytic cells, pencils, dry cells/battery, connecting wire, distilled water, acid, H2SO4, CuSO4, ZnSO4, NaCl, KCl; graph/sand papers, Li-ion and lead-acid cells, copper ores/sheets, aluminium ores, gold, zinc, ammeter, voltmeter"],"assessment":[],"periods":"68 (shared, under 1.1) + 58 (shared, under 2.1)"},{"topic":"Chemical Kinetics, Equilibrium and Energetics","mcCode":"1.0","mc":"Demonstrate mastery of basic concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry","scCode":"1.1","sc":"Demonstrate mastery of concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry","activities":["Placed here because rates of reaction, chemical equilibrium and reaction energetics are core chemical theories/principles falling under Specific Competence 1.1. However, no learning activity on reaction rates, equilibrium, or energetics/thermochemistry (exothermic/endothermic reactions, enthalpy) appears in any of the four Form tables supplied, so the detailed Learning Activities, Methods and Resources are not available from this source — please share the relevant pages so this topic can be completed accurately rather than inferred."],"methods":["Placed here because rates of reaction, chemical equilibrium and reaction energetics are core chemical theories/principles falling under Specific Competence 1.1. However, no learning activity on reaction rates, equilibrium, or energetics/thermochemistry (exothermic/endothermic reactions, enthalpy) appears in any of the four Form tables supplied, so the detailed Learning Activities, Methods and Resources are not available from this source — please share the relevant pages so this topic can be completed accurately rather than inferred."],"resources":["Placed here because rates of reaction, chemical equilibrium and reaction energetics are core chemical theories/principles falling under Specific Competence 1.1. However, no learning activity on reaction rates, equilibrium, or energetics/thermochemistry (exothermic/endothermic reactions, enthalpy) appears in any of the four Form tables supplied, so the detailed Learning Activities, Methods and Resources are not available from this source — please share the relevant pages so this topic can be completed accurately rather than inferred."],"assessment":[],"periods":""},{"topic":"Extraction and Properties of Metals","mcCode":"4.0","mc":"Demonstrate mastery of basic principles of extraction of metals","scCode":"4.1","sc":"Demonstrate mastery of the principles of extraction of metals","activities":["Describe the extraction of iron from its ore (ores, stages, and methods of extraction)"],"methods":["Field trips to nearby mining sites/factories to analyse the iron extraction process, followed by a written report","Group discussion describing the stages and methods of metal extraction"],"resources":["Wall charts showing metal ores and their extraction methods, and real ores for metals present in Tanzania","Note: the source content here addresses extraction of iron specifically; a separate, dedicated treatment of general 'properties of metals' is not present alongside it in this document."],"assessment":[],"periods":"10"},{"topic":"Compounds of Metal Part I (Oxides, Hydroxides, Carbonates and Hydrogen Carbonates)","mcCode":"1.0","mc":"Demonstrate mastery of basic concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.0 Conduct experiments in Chemistry","scCode":"1.1","sc":"Demonstrate mastery of concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.1 Conduct experiments in Chemistry","activities":["Describe the preparation and properties of oxides, hydroxides, carbonates and hydrogen carbonates of metals, relating them to metal extraction, paint-making, textiles, drinking water, soaps and detergents","Prepare various compounds of metals in the laboratory"],"methods":["Inquiry-based learning investigating properties of metal compounds","Group discussion examining different compounds of metals, followed by laboratory preparation experiments"],"resources":["Periodic table, lead metal, iron strips, magnesium nitrate, zinc sulphate, lead nitrate, copper sulphate, silver nitrate, magnesium ribbon, aluminium foil, laboratory glassware, water trough, flasks, gas jars, Kipp's apparatus, combustion tube, heat sources, hydrochloric acid, sulphuric acid","Note: the syllabus treats oxides/hydroxides/carbonates/hydrogen carbonates together with nitrates/chlorides/sulphates as a single combined activity, rather than as two separate Parts. 'Compounds of Metal Part II' below draws on this same combined activity and period allocation."],"assessment":[],"periods":"68 (shared, under 1.1) + 58 (shared, under 2.1)"},{"topic":"Compounds of Metal Part II (Nitrates, Chlorides and Sulphates)","mcCode":"1.0","mc":"Demonstrate mastery of basic concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.0 Conduct experiments in Chemistry","scCode":"1.1","sc":"Demonstrate mastery of concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.1 Conduct experiments in Chemistry","activities":["Describe the preparation and properties of nitrates, chlorides and sulphates of metals","Prepare various compounds of metals in the laboratory"],"methods":["Inquiry-based learning investigating properties of metal compounds","Group discussion examining different compounds of metals, followed by laboratory preparation experiments"],"resources":["Periodic table, lead metal, iron strips, magnesium nitrate, zinc sulphate, lead nitrate, copper sulphate, silver nitrate; laboratory glassware, gas jars, Kipp's apparatus, hydrochloric acid, sulphuric acid","Same single combined source activity as 'Compounds of Metal Part I' — see note there."],"assessment":[],"periods":"68 (shared, under 1.1) + 58 (shared, under 2.1)"}],[{"topic":"Non-Metals and their Compounds","mcCode":"1.0","mc":"Demonstrate mastery of basic concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.0 Conduct experiments in Chemistry","scCode":"1.1","sc":"Demonstrate mastery of concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.1 Conduct experiments in Chemistry","activities":["Describe the oxidizing and displacement properties of non-metal compounds (oxygen, hydrogen, chlorine, hydrogen chloride, sulphur dioxide, nitrogen, ammonia, and carbon dioxide)","Demonstrate oxidizing and displacement properties of non-metallic compounds experimentally","Prepare sulphuric acid from sulphur dioxide"],"methods":["Group discussion describing properties of non-metal compounds, with a project visiting industries making gases/pesticides/fertilisers","Inquiry-based learning investigating industrial mechanisms involving oxidizing/displacement properties of non-metals","Experimentation preparing sulphuric acid from sulphur dioxide"],"resources":["Wall charts and pictures of oxidation reactions, periodic table, oxygen/hydrogen/chlorine/hydrogen chloride/sulphur dioxide/nitrogen/ammonia/carbon dioxide gases, coloured flower dyes, hydrogen sulphide, iron(II) chloride, potassium dichromate, potassium permanganate","Note: in the syllabus document received, this content is placed under Form Three (Specific Competences 1.1(g) and 2.1(e)–(f)), not Form Four. Presented here under the Form Four heading as given, with this placement flagged for verification."],"assessment":[],"periods":"68 (Form Three total, shared) + 58 (Form Three total, shared)"},{"topic":"Introduction to Organic Chemistry","mcCode":"1.0","mc":"Demonstrate mastery of basic concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.0 Demonstrate mastery of basic terminologies in Chemistry","scCode":"1.1","sc":"Demonstrate mastery of concepts, theories and principles in Chemistry / 2.1 Use the IUPAC nomenclature to name chemical species / 2.2 Use chemical symbols, formulae and equations to represent chemical reactions","activities":["Explore the basic tenets of Organic Chemistry (organic compounds; aliphatic hydrocarbons and alcohols)","Use Chemistry software to draw organic structures of hydrocarbons and alcohols (1 to 10 carbon atoms)","Name aliphatic hydrocarbons and alcohols with 1 to 10 carbon atoms using the IUPAC system and Chemistry software","Describe the chemical reactions of aliphatic hydrocarbons and alcohols in relation to their structures"],"methods":["Brainstorming on the concept of Organic Chemistry, plus group discussion on categories of hydrocarbons/alcohols","Experimentation using Chemistry software (e.g., ChemDraw-type tools) to draw organic structures","Group discussion and laboratory observation of labelled containers to identify/name compounds using IUPAC rules","Gallery walk, roleplay and group discussion describing reactions of aliphatic hydrocarbons and alcohols"],"resources":["Molecular models and organic substances present in the environment; Chemistry-based computer software; wall charts showing structures of hydrocarbons, molecular model kits, labelled containers of hydrocarbons and alcohols"],"assessment":[],"periods":"36 (shared across the three related activities in this competence group)"},{"topic":"Pollution","mcCode":"5.0","mc":"